// does NOT change here any more (see step 6 above) — it keeps the
// exact same overlaid position at every width, so nothing about the
// text jumps or shifts when this breakpoint hits; only the image
// crops in underneath it. `.education-clouds-wrap` doesn't need its
// own media query either (see step 13's history entry): its height is
// `64.5%` of `.education-visual`, the same measured courtyard-floor
// percentage `.content` already uses — so it automatically re-scales
// together with `.education-visual` at this breakpoint exactly the
// same way `.content`'s own percentages do, rather than needing a
// separate override here.
// The percentages on `.content` still land correctly in the gap even
// against the cropped image — worked out by hand: `object-position:
// center` crops the image symmetrically around its own middle, and
// `.content`'s `left: 47%` is already close enough to that midpoint
// (47% vs. 50%) that the remapping from "percentage of the cropped
// box" back to "percentage of the original image" barely moves it —
// nowhere near enough to visibly drift off the gap.
//
// History of this breakpoint's crop, if you ever want to revisit any
// of it: `aspect-ratio: 1 / 1` originally (step 5) — chosen so the
// crop fraction stayed identical on every phone (~72% of the image's
// width visible, ~14% cropped per side); replaced with a bare
// `height: 100vh` (step 15) to match ArtIslandSection's full-screen
// look, which cropped MUCH tighter (~33% visible) but, on short/wide
// windows within this breakpoint, started cropping the image's
// top/bottom instead of its sides — an unwanted regression; replaced
// with THIS version's `min-height: 75vh` + `aspect-ratio: 5000 / 3600`
// pairing (step 16), which keeps ArtIslandSection's "fills the screen,
// crops the sides" look on true phones while both raising how much of
// the sides show (~44% visible) and permanently ruling out the
// top/bottom crop regardless of window shape.
//
// Why `1024px` and not the usual `640px`: first shipped at `640px` to
// match the rest of the family, but per your follow-up you were
// testing by dragging a desktop browser window narrower and never saw
// the crop appear — at `640px` that's expected (a browser window has
// to get genuinely phone-narrow before the swap happens), but you
// wanted it to show up sooner, without narrowing all the way to phone
// width. `1024px` is comfortably inside typical "half a laptop screen"
// window-dragging territory, so the crop shows up well before the
// window gets anywhere near actual phone width. This only changes the
// breakpoint for THIS component — every other section in the family
// keeps its own `640px` mobile breakpoint as-is.
//
// Full-bleed technique: same break-out-of-the-container trick as
// OtherIslandSection.astro —
//   width: 100vw; position: relative; left: 50%; right: 50%;
//   margin-left: -50vw; margin-right: -50vw;
// — applied to `.education-image-wrap` (and to `.education-clouds-wrap`
// too, so the clouds layer spans the exact same full-bleed width as
// the image sitting in front of it, edge to edge). The
// horizontal-scrollbar-width edge case this can introduce is already
// handled page-wide by `overflow-x: hidden` on `html` in Layout.astro,
// so nothing extra is needed here for that.
//
// Height (desktop/default): no `object-fit`/`aspect-ratio` cropping
// trick — the image is `width: 100%; height: auto;`, so it always
// keeps its own aspect ratio (measured 5000x3600, ~1.39:1) at whatever
// the full page width happens to be, with NO cropping, ever. Per your
// earlier note that it's fine either way, this leans on ordinary block
// flow: the image is real in-flow content inside the wrap, so on a
// short/wide viewport where it renders taller than one screen, the
// section (a plain flex column sized by its own content) just grows to
// fit it, same "grow instead of crop" spirit as the `aspect-ratio`
// trick used elsewhere in this family.
//
// No foreground bob loop or mouse-repel script in this file — those
// fit small floating islands, not one full-bleed illustration (also,
// this image is exactly 100vw wide with nothing extra to shift into; a
// mouse-repel translate() would expose a gap of the flat background
// color at whichever edge it moved away from). Safe to leave this
// file's copy of the shared parallax script out entirely: the other
// three copies still on the page (Art/Computer/Achievement Island
// Section) already drive every remaining `.parallax-inner` element
// site-wide. The clouds loop (step 8) is plain CSS `animation`, not
// this shared script, for the same reason the glitch bursts are plain
// CSS too — a simple one-property loop doesn't need JS to drive it.
//
// No `overflow: hidden` here (except `.education-clouds-wrap`, which
// needs its own local `overflow: hidden` — see the comment above it —
// purely to crop its doubled-up scrolling track back down to the
// wrap's own box; that's unrelated to the page-wide horizontal-bleed
// guard described next). See the comment on `overflow-x: hidden` on
// `html` in Layout.astro. That handles horizontal clipping page-wide,
// so this section isn't hard-clipped if it ever needs more vertical
// room than one screen (see above).
//
// Still carries `.sky-chain` (seam-matching gap-collapse) and
// `.halftone-hover` (page-wide cursor-spotlight, purely by having the
// class — no markup/script needed here for it), same as before.
//
// Clouds background (`cloudsBackground`, step 8, reprocessed in step
// 9, loop fixed in step 10, sizing fixed in steps 11–13): a single wide
// clouds illustration, doubled up side-by-side inside a `200%`-wide
// flex track and scrolled with one CSS `animation`, the same
// "duplicate + translateX" loop technique used for any seamless
// horizontal marquee — this ONLY reads as seamless because (a) the
// source file itself was made to tile horizontally first (see step
// 9's history entry), AND (b) each copy renders at its own natural
// width-mapped aspect ratio rather than being `object-fit: cover`-
// cropped (see step 10's history entry) — a CSS loop technique alone
// can't paper over a source image whose edges don't match, and
// cropping the source's width per copy defeats even a perfectly
// tileable source.
// `.education-clouds-track` holds two `<img>` copies of the exact same
// file, each pinned to `width: 50%` (i.e. exactly one wrap-width each)
// so the two sit edge-to-edge with no gap, and `height: auto` so each
// renders at its own natural aspect ratio, never cropped horizontally.
// The keyframes animate `transform` from `translateX(-50%)` to
// `translateX(0%)` — since the percentage in a `translateX` on the
// track resolves against the TRACK's own width (200% of the wrap),
// `-50%` of that is exactly one wrap-width, so animating UP to `0%`
// slides the track rightward by exactly one wrap-width over the loop.
// Concretely: at the start, copy 2 fills the visible wrap and copy 1
// sits one wrap-width to its left, off-screen; as the track slides
// right, copy 1 enters from the left edge and copy 2 exits off the
// right edge — i.e. any given cloud shape visibly travels from left to
// right across the section, per your request. Once the track finishes
// sliding (copy 1 now exactly fills the wrap, in the same position
// copy 2 started in), the `infinite` animation restarts at `-50%` —
// which, because copy 1 and copy 2 are pixel-identical AND each now
// shows its full, genuinely-tileable width (step 10), is visually
// indistinguishable from where the previous cycle just ended, so the
// loop reads as continuous with no visible seam or snap. `linear`
// easing keeps the drift at a constant speed rather than easing
// in/out at the loop boundary, which would otherwise read as a
// stutter every cycle. `.education-clouds-track-row` (see step 13's
// history entry) needs its own `overflow: hidden` so only one
// wrap-width of the always-200%-wide track is ever visible at a time —
// without it the second copy would spill out past the section's own
// edges instead of being clipped by them. `.education-clouds-fill`
// (also step 13) is a plain white sibling below the track row, sized
// via `flex: 1 1 auto` to fill whatever's left of the wrap's `64.5%`
// total height once the clouds row has taken what it needs — see step
// 13's history entry for why that total is `64.5%` specifically. The
// outer `.education-clouds-wrap`'s own `overflow: hidden` is what
// clips the whole column back down to that `64.5%` box. It sits BEHIND
// `.education-image-wrap` purely by DOM order
// (it's the first child of `.education-visual`, and neither it nor the
// plain `<img>` inside `.education-image-wrap` sets a `z-index`, so
// normal painting order already puts it underneath) — no `z-index`
// needed, same as how `.education-image-wrap`'s own `<img>` doesn't
// need one to sit below `.content`/the glitch frames, which DO set
// one. Not gated behind `prefers-reduced-motion`, for the same reason
// the glitch bursts below aren't: Firefox and Brave both report
// "reduce" by default for privacy reasons unrelated to the viewer's
// actual motion preference, which would otherwise silently hide this
// on a CV meant to be seen by whoever an employer happens to open it
// with. Entirely optional — like the glitch-square props, it only
// renders (and only pays for the extra `<img>` requests) when
// `cloudsBackground` is actually passed in.
//
// Glitch "burst" spots (`glitchSquare1Frame1/2/3`,
// `glitchSquare2Frame1/2/3` — each group of three optional, but only
// renders if all three in that group are given): each is a full
// illustration-canvas-sized, mostly-transparent image (matching
// `image`'s own aspect ratio) with a grayscale noise texture baked
// into ONE specific small spot and everything else transparent — the
// exact same technique (and the exact same two spots) the very first
// version of this feature used (see history item 10) — so each just
// needs `inset: 0` + `object-fit: cover`/`object-position: center` to
// line up pixel-for-pixel with the base illustration at EVERY
// breakpoint, including the narrow-window crop, with no separate
// positioning math and no risk of ever drifting into `.content`'s box
// (unlike the previous, CSS-positioned "burst zone" phase — see item
// 10 for why that mattered). Three frames per square, stacked and
// flickered through in rapid succession, briefly, every so often.
//
// This went through several shapes based on your feedback, worth
// knowing about if you ever want to revisit any of them:
//   1. Started as a periodic flash (`opacity: 0` at rest, a JS
//      `setTimeout` scheduler adding/removing an `.is-glitching` class
//      at random intervals) reusing AchievementIslandSection's
//      `clip-path`-banding `glitch-pop` effect — dropped because it
//      wasn't rendering reliably for you across browsers.
//   2. Rebuilt as a direct port of HobbyIslandSection.astro's
//      Monado-symbol swap instead (opacity animated INSIDE the
//      keyframes with `forwards` fill, rather than a separate static
//      `opacity` rule racing the animation on specificity) — this
//      part rendered reliably from here on.
//   3. Per "more glitchy and less flashy... shakes a bit left and
//      right," swapped the brightness pulse for a `translateX` shake.
//   4. Per "the moving left and right isn't the best... make the
//      color range change more, a bit more flashy," dropped the shake
//      for a much wider `hue-rotate`/`saturate`/`brightness` swing.
//   5. Per "make it glitchy over it, and maybe so that it doesn't
//      disappear but is present to be seen all the time": `opacity: 1`
//      became the PERMANENT base state — no more show/hide at all, so
//      no more JS scheduler either (there's no `<script>` block in
//      this file). Each square runs its own never-ending CSS
//      `animation` that sits at `filter: none` for most of every ~4s
//      cycle and pops through the color swing briefly near the end,
//      forever — `glitchImage2` offset by half a cycle so the two
//      don't pop in sync. The square's PRESENCE stopped flickering
//      entirely; only its color did, periodically.
//   6. Added an actual per-pixel warp on top of the existing color
//      swing, per "make be a twirl ripple effect that is played from
//      time to time for the squares, like a glitching screen" — an SVG
//      `feTurbulence`/`feDisplacementMap` filter (same technique
//      HobbyIslandSection.astro's fountain tried in its own v22, just
//      put to different use here) plus a smoothly-eased
//      `transform: rotate()/scale()` wobble running alongside it.
//      REJECTED after you saw it, with two reference images attached:
//      "hmm thank you but i was thinking of something like this. the
//      effect should still look like a square for the most part." The
//      warp made the whole silhouette blobby/distorted, which is the
//      opposite of what your reference images show (a square staying
//      mostly intact, with thin streaks bleeding out of it) — see the
//      next entry for the replacement.
//   7. Removed the feTurbulence/feDisplacementMap warp and the
//      rotate/scale twirl entirely (along with the SVG `<defs>` markup
//      and the `--warp-filter` custom property that powered them) and
//      replaced both with a "slice tear" effect instead. The
//      always-visible base square keeps ONLY its plain color swing
//      (unwarped, unrotated — this is what keeps the silhouette
//      looking "like a square for the most part"). On top of it, each
//      glitch image gets several duplicate copies (`GLITCH_SLICES`,
//      see below) cropped via `clip-path` to a thin horizontal band
//      and independently `translateX()`-jumped. At rest a slice sits
//      pixel-perfectly over the base (identical image content, so it's
//      invisible as its own layer) and only reads as a distinct offset
//      streak during its jump — since each band is cropped to the
//      square's own footprint, this shows up as a thin horizontal tear
//      bleeding sideways out of an intact square, matching the "streaks
//      off the edges of a mostly-square shape" look in your reference
//      images, rather than an organic warped blob.
//   8. Per "not bad but it feels kinda forces/not random... could you
//      make the effect a bit more random please how it moves, maybe a
//      shaky line": the previous version had every slice sharing one
//      fixed 4s clock and one fixed set of keyframe percentages, so
//      all five always jumped at the exact same instants relative to
//      each other, cycle after cycle — mechanically perfect repetition
//      read as "forced." Now each slice runs its OWN `--slice-duration`
//      (a different loop length per slice, see `GLITCH_SLICES`) and
//      its own `--slice-delay`, so they drift in and out of phase with
//      each other continuously — which slices happen to be mid-jump,
//      and by how much, keeps changing rather than repeating
//      identically. The keyframes themselves
//      (`@keyframes education-glitch-slice`) also got many more,
//      smaller, alternating-direction steps instead of a handful of
//      big clean jumps, so a slice reads as vibrating/rattling ("shaky
//      line") rather than snapping to one offset and holding still.
//   9. Per you sending a reference image of a full-frame RGB/datamosh-
//      style corruption burst (saturated red/green/blue/cyan/magenta/
//      yellow/black/white block noise) and asking "is it possible to
//      make a glitch effect like this?" — I flagged that this look is
//      a different CHARACTER of effect entirely (loud, full-color,
//      chaotic) from the small muted-gray square this feature had been
//      up to this point, and asked how to scope it; you picked "let
//      the glitch spread beyond the small square into a larger, more
//      dominant burst." Everything from items 1-8 above (the small
//      square, its permanent color swing, the slice-tear streaks) was
//      GONE — replaced with two bigger rectangular zones sitting
//      mostly invisible over the illustration and periodically
//      flickering through three procedurally-generated noise-texture
//      images in rapid, staggered succession for well under a second,
//      plus a brief erratic `translate()` shake on the whole zone —
//      then vanishing again until the next burst. This DID introduce a
//      real bug, caught with Playwright before shipping: at
//      narrow/mobile widths, `.content`'s `width: min(32ch, 100%)` (a
//      fixed CHARACTER count, not a percentage) becomes a much larger
//      FRACTION of the narrower wrap, so a zone placed where it was
//      safely clear of `.content` at the default full-bleed width
//      ended up visually overlapping it once the screen narrowed
//      enough — fixed at the time by moving both zones below
//      `.content`'s vertical range at every width, but this whole
//      class of bug goes away entirely with item 10's approach below.
//  10. THIS VERSION (glitch history), per "could this effect be
//      applied only for the two of my squares/their position please?
//      and maybe that instead of color it is made up off different
//      shades of gray, black and white please?": two changes. First,
//      back to the ORIGINAL small-square positions/sizes from this
//      feature's very first version (measured off `gray_square_1.webp`/
//      `gray_square_2.webp`'s own alpha channels, back when this
//      feature started) — the CSS-positioned "bigger burst zone" from
//      item 9 is gone, and with it, the whole class of narrow-width
//      overlap bug that zone introduced, since a full-canvas image
//      sharing the base illustration's own `object-fit`/`object-
//      position` rules crops in lockstep with it automatically at
//      every breakpoint, the same way `.education-glitch`/
//      `.education-glitch-slice` always did in earlier versions —
//      no hand-measured CSS rectangle to ever drift out of place.
//      Second, the noise-frame images themselves (regenerated) now
//      use a strictly grayscale palette (black, white, and several
//      shades of gray in between) instead of saturated RGB colors —
//      same generation technique as item 9's frames (random-width
//      horizontal bands subdivided into vertical blocks, per-pixel
//      static, thin streak lines) just restricted to one palette and,
//      this time, painted only inside each square's own small bbox on
//      an otherwise-transparent canvas rather than across a whole
//      standalone rectangle. Six frame props now instead of three
//      (`glitchSquare1Frame1/2/3` for the first square's spot,
//      `glitchSquare2Frame1/2/3` for the second's) since each square
//      needs its OWN three frames baked at its OWN bbox — sharing one
//      frame image between both squares would make them always
//      flicker in lockstep instead of independently. The flicker and
//      shake mechanics themselves (rapid staggered on/off blips,
//      brief erratic `translate()`) are otherwise unchanged from
//      item 9.
//
// `z-index: 1` on `.education-glitch-frame` matters for the same
// reason `.content` needs it — see the `.halftone-spotlight` comment
// in Layout.astro: without it, the page-wide cursor-spotlight would
// paint over these and mute them depending on cursor position. Not
// gated behind `prefers-reduced-motion` — Firefox and Brave both
// report "reduce" by default for privacy reasons unrelated to the
// viewer's actual motion preference, which would otherwise silently
// hide this on a CV meant to be seen by whoever an employer happens to
// open it with. Each square's three frames only render if all three
// of ITS OWN props are supplied (a partial set for one square renders
// nothing for that square, rather than a broken one- or two-frame
// flicker; the other square is unaffected either way).
//
// Usage:
//   <EducationIslandSection
//     id="section-education"
//     eyebrow="Island 03"
//     title="Adipiscing Elit Sed"
//     text="Sunt in culpa qui officia deserunt mollit anim id est laborum..."
//     image="/images/education_island_ver3.webp"
//     cloudsBackground="/images/education_island_clouds_background.webp"
//     glitchSquare1Frame1="/images/glitch_gray_sq1_1.webp"
//     glitchSquare1Frame2="/images/glitch_gray_sq1_2.webp"
//     glitchSquare1Frame3="/images/glitch_gray_sq1_3.webp"
//     glitchSquare2Frame1="/images/glitch_gray_sq2_1.webp"
//     glitchSquare2Frame2="/images/glitch_gray_sq2_2.webp"
//     glitchSquare2Frame3="/images/glitch_gray_sq2_3.webp"
//   />

interface Props {
  id: string;
  eyebrow?: string;
  title: string;
  text: string;
  image: string;
  alt?: string;
  cloudsBackground?: string;
  glitchSquare1Frame1?: string;
  glitchSquare1Frame2?: string;
  glitchSquare1Frame3?: string;
  glitchSquare2Frame1?: string;
  glitchSquare2Frame2?: string;
  glitchSquare2Frame3?: string;
}

const {
  id,
  eyebrow,
  title,
  text,
  image,
  alt = "",
  cloudsBackground,
  glitchSquare1Frame1,
  glitchSquare1Frame2,
  glitchSquare1Frame3,
  glitchSquare2Frame1,
  glitchSquare2Frame2,
  glitchSquare2Frame3,
} = Astro.props;
---

<section class="education-section sky-chain halftone-hover" id={id}>
  <div class="education-visual">
    {cloudsBackground && (
      <div class="education-clouds-wrap" aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="education-clouds-track-row">
          <div class="education-clouds-track">
            <img src={cloudsBackground} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
            <img src={cloudsBackground} alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="education-clouds-fill"></div>
      </div>
    )}

    <div class="education-image-wrap">
      <img src={image} alt={alt} loading="lazy" decoding="async" />

      {glitchSquare1Frame1 && glitchSquare1Frame2 && glitchSquare1Frame3 && (
        <>
          <img class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-1 education-glitch-frame-a" src={glitchSquare1Frame1} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <img class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-1 education-glitch-frame-b" src={glitchSquare1Frame2} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <img class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-1 education-glitch-frame-c" src={glitchSquare1Frame3} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </>
      )}
      {glitchSquare2Frame1 && glitchSquare2Frame2 && glitchSquare2Frame3 && (
        <>
          <img class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-2 education-glitch-frame-a" src={glitchSquare2Frame1} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <img class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-2 education-glitch-frame-b" src={glitchSquare2Frame2} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <img class="education-glitch-frame education-glitch-frame-group-2 education-glitch-frame-c" src={glitchSquare2Frame3} alt="" aria-hidden="true" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
        </>
      )}
    </div>

    <div class="content">
      {eyebrow && <p class="eyebrow">{eyebrow}</p>}
      <h2>{title}</h2>
      <p class="body-text">{text}</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .education-section {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 100vh;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: clamp(3rem, 8vw, 7rem) clamp(1.5rem, 6vw, 6rem);
    /* Flat color, kept from an earlier request — still the fallback
       behind the illustration's transparent gap (see the top-of-file
       comment) for the moment before `cloudsBackground` loads in, and
       for callers that don't pass one at all. */
    background: #40CAEC;
  }

  /* Positioning context for `.content` and `.education-clouds-wrap`
     (see the top-of-file comment for why both live here as siblings of
     the image wrap rather than nested inside it). Otherwise a plain,
     unstyled block — it just wraps the pieces so their percentages
     have something stable to resolve against. */
  .education-visual {
    position: relative;
  }

  /* Looping clouds layer, behind everything else — see the dedicated
     top-of-file comment ("Clouds background") for the full history and
     mechanics. Same full-bleed breakout as `.education-image-wrap`
     below. Height is `64.5%` of `.education-visual` — measured
     directly off `education_island_ver3.webp`'s own alpha channel
     (see step 13's history entry), matching exactly where the
     courtyard floor turns opaque, the SAME percentage `.content`'s own
     vertical bounds already use — so the wrap always reaches down to
     that line, at any breakpoint, without needing its own media query
     (see the top-of-file "Narrow-window" comment). `position: absolute`
     still contributes no height to `.education-visual` itself, same
     trick `.content` uses. A column flex container with two stacked
     children (step 13): the clouds themselves, sized to their own
     natural height only and never stretched or cropped (so step 10's
     tileable-seam fix stays intact), and a plain white fill below them
     that grows to soak up whatever's left of this `64.5%` box — see
     `.education-clouds-track-row` and `.education-clouds-fill` below.
     `overflow: hidden` clips the whole column back down to this box.
     Sits behind `.education-image-wrap` purely by DOM order — no
     `z-index` needed, see the top-of-file comment. */
  .education-clouds-wrap {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    height: 64.5%;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  /* Sized to its own content only (`flex: 0 0 auto`) — exactly as
     tall as the clouds artwork renders at, whatever that works out to
     at the current viewport width, never stretched to fill the rest
     of `.education-clouds-wrap`. Its own `overflow: hidden` clips the
     always-200%-wide scrolling track back down to one wrap-width. */
  .education-clouds-track-row {
    flex: 0 0 auto;
    width: 100%;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Two copies of the same image, edge-to-edge, slid rightward on a
     continuous loop — see the top-of-file comment for exactly how the
     `-50% → 0%` keyframes below work out to "clouds drift left to
     right, seamlessly." */
  .education-clouds-track {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start; /* don't let images get flex-stretched vertically */
    width: 200%;
    animation: education-clouds-scroll 60s linear infinite;
  }

  .education-clouds-track img {
    display: block;
    flex: 0 0 50%;
    width: 50%;
    height: auto;   /* natural aspect ratio — full source width per copy, never cropped; see step 10's history entry */
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Grows to fill whatever vertical space is left in
     `.education-clouds-wrap` once the clouds row above has taken what
     it needs (`flex: 1 1 auto`) — see step 13's history entry for why
     this is a separate box (rather than one shared background behind
     both the clouds row and this space) and why the wrap's total is
     `64.5%` specifically. `margin-top: -1vw` (step 14) pulls it up to
     deliberately overlap the bottom edge of `.education-clouds-track-
     row` by a small, fixed amount — since this element comes AFTER
     the track row in DOM order, its opaque white paints on top of
     that overlap, guaranteeing there's never a razor-thin gap for the
     flat `#40CAEC` behind everything to show through at the seam,
     regardless of sub-pixel rounding or which clouds image ends up
     passed in as `cloudsBackground`. See step 14's history entry. */
  .education-clouds-fill {
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    margin-top: -1vw;
    background: #ffffff;
  }

  @keyframes education-clouds-scroll {
    from { transform: translateX(-50%); }
    to { transform: translateX(0%); }
  }

  /* Full-bleed break-out: spans the full viewport width no matter what
     this section's own left/right padding is — same trick as
     OtherIslandSection.astro (see the component-level comment above).
     `line-height: 0` avoids the couple of stray px an inline <img>
     otherwise leaves below itself. */
  .education-image-wrap {
    position: relative;
    left: 50%;
    right: 50%;
    width: 100vw;
    margin-left: -50vw;
    margin-right: -50vw;
    line-height: 0;
  }

  .education-image-wrap img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* Glitch "burst" spots — v10 (see the top-of-file comment, glitch
     overlays history item 10). Back to the ORIGINAL small-square
     positions/sizes this feature started with: each `<img>` here is a
     full illustration-canvas-sized, mostly-transparent image (a
     grayscale noise texture baked into one small spot, matching
     `gray_square_1.webp`/`gray_square_2.webp`'s own alpha-channel
     bounds from this feature's very first version), so `inset: 0` +
     `object-fit: cover`/`object-position: center` below line it up
     pixel-for-pixel with the base illustration automatically, at
     every breakpoint — including the narrow-window crop, since it
     crops in lockstep with the base image rather than needing its own
     hand-measured CSS rectangle (that was the previous, `v9` "burst
     zone" approach, and the source of a real narrow-width overlap bug
     with `.content` — see item 9's entry for what happened and why
     this approach doesn't have that problem). Mostly `opacity: 0` (see
     `education-glitch-burst-flicker` below) — only one of a square's
     three frames is ever meant to be visible at a time, taking turns. */
  .education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-frame {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 1;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center;
    opacity: 0;
    animation:
      education-glitch-burst-flicker var(--burst-duration) steps(1, end) infinite,
      education-glitch-burst-shake var(--burst-duration) steps(30, end) infinite;
  }

  /* Each square's three frames share one `--burst-duration` (so their
     flicker and shake stay in sync with EACH OTHER), but the two
     squares' durations differ from one another so the two squares'
     bursts drift in and out of phase — same "don't let independent
     things read as one synced thing" idea used everywhere else in
     this file, down to the bob-timing offsets in
     HobbyIslandSection.astro. */
  .education-glitch-frame-group-1 {
    --burst-duration: 6.3s;
  }
  .education-glitch-frame-group-2 {
    --burst-duration: 7.1s;
  }

  /* Frame `a` uses the base rule's default `animation-delay` (`0s` for
     both animations, unstated). Frames `b`/`c` get a slightly earlier
     negative delay on ONLY the first (flicker) animation in the list —
     the second value, `0s`, re-states the shake's delay so it stays
     identical across all three frames of a group (only one frame is
     ever opaque at a time, so it doesn't matter that all three "shake"
     on the same schedule — what you actually see is just whichever
     frame is currently visible rattling in place). This staggering is
     what turns three otherwise-identical on/off blips into a rapid
     a-then-b-then-c flicker instead of all three flashing in unison.
     Compound selectors (`.education-glitch-frame.education-glitch-
     frame-b`, not just the bare `-b` class) for the same specificity
     reason documented at length elsewhere in this file's history: the
     base `.education-glitch-frame` rule above sets the `animation`
     SHORTHAND, which would reset a lower-specificity override's
     `animation-delay` back to `0s, 0s`. */
  .education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-frame.education-glitch-frame-b {
    animation-delay: -0.18s, 0s;
  }
  .education-image-wrap img.education-glitch-frame.education-glitch-frame-c {
    animation-delay: -0.36s, 0s;
  }

  /* Invisible for the vast majority of the loop, with two rapid on/off
     "blips" packed into the last ~10% of it. Combined with the three
     frames' staggered delays above, this chains into a rapid
     multi-flash burst (a-on, b-on, c-on, a-on again, briefly, ...)
     lasting well under half a second, then silence again for the rest
     of the multi-second cycle — the "burst" itself. `steps(1, end)`
     (not the jump-cut `steps(26, end)`/`steps(30, end)` used
     elsewhere in this file) is deliberate: with only two states (0 or
     1) there's nothing for extra steps to subdivide — one hard cut per
     keyframe boundary is all a plain `opacity: 0 → 1 → 0` needs. */
  @keyframes education-glitch-burst-flicker {
    0%   { opacity: 0; }
    84%  { opacity: 0; }
    85%  { opacity: 1; }
    87%  { opacity: 0; }
    89%  { opacity: 1; }
    91%  { opacity: 0; }
    100% { opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* A brief, erratic `translate()` shake, timed to the same ~84%-93%
     window the frame flicker above uses, so whichever frame is
     currently visible visibly rattles while it's flickering rather
     than flickering perfectly still in place — the same "shaky line"
     idea from the previous (small-square) version of this feature,
     carried over and applied here too. Since this now animates
     `transform` on the same full-canvas `<img>` the flicker's
     `opacity` animates (rather than a separate wrapping box, as the
     `v9` "burst zone" version used), a shift of a few px moves the
     WHOLE mostly-transparent canvas — but since only the small baked-
     in noise spot is ever visible, that spot itself still visibly
     shakes by the same few px, same as before. `steps(30, end)` for
     the same jump-cut reasoning as the rest of this file's glitch
     animations. */
  @keyframes education-glitch-burst-shake {
    0%   { transform: translate(0, 0); }
    84%  { transform: translate(0, 0); }
    93%  { transform: translate(0, 0); }
    100% { transform: translate(0, 0); }
  }

  /* Positioned inside the courtyard gap between the two buildings —
     see the top-of-file comment for how these percentages were
     measured off the source image's alpha channel and cross-checked
     against your reference photo. Percentages here resolve against
     `.education-visual`'s own box, which matches the image wrap's box
     exactly at this breakpoint (see the top-of-file comment for why),
     so the text stays centered in the gap at every viewport width. */
  .content {
    position: absolute;
    top: 33%;
    left: 47%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    z-index: 1;

    width: min(32ch, 100%);
    text-align: left;
  }

  .eyebrow {
